// Regular 2D array — 3 rows, each with 4 columns
int[][] matrix = new int[3][4];

// Jagged array — rows have DIFFERENT lengths
int[][] jagged = new int[3][];
jagged[0] = new int[]{1};
jagged[1] = new int[]{2, 3};
jagged[2] = new int[]{4, 5, 6};

// Access jagged array
for (int[] row : jagged) {
    System.out.println(Arrays.toString(row));
}
// [1]
// [2, 3]
// [4, 5, 6]

System.out.println(jagged.length);     // 3 (number of rows)
System.out.println(jagged[1].length);  // 2 (length of row 1)
```

```java
int[] arr = {10, 20, 30};

System.out.println(arr[0]);  // 10 — valid
System.out.println(arr[2]);  // 30 — valid (last index)
System.out.println(arr[3]);  // ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException! index 3 >= length 3
System.out.println(arr[-1]); // ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException! negative index

// Classic off-by-one bug:
for (int i = 0; i <= arr.length; i++) {  // BUG: should be i < arr.length
    System.out.println(arr[i]);  // fails when i == 3
}

// Safe loop:
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {   // correct: strict less-than
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}
```

```java
// Array — fixed size, primitives, performance-critical
int[] scores = new int[100];
scores[0] = 95;  // O(1) access

// ArrayList — variable size, need add/remove
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("Alice");
names.add("Bob");
names.remove(0);          // shifts elements
System.out.println(names.size());

// ArrayList can't hold primitives directly
ArrayList<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>();  // Integer, not int
nums.add(42);  // autoboxing: int 42 → Integer(42)
```

```java
void doubleValues(int[] arr) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        arr[i] *= 2;  // modifies the ORIGINAL array
    }
}

void tryReassign(int[] arr) {
    arr = new int[]{99, 99};  // only changes LOCAL variable — caller unaffected
}

int[] nums = {1, 2, 3};
doubleValues(nums);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums));  // [2, 4, 6] — changed!

tryReassign(nums);
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums));  // [2, 4, 6] — unchanged
```

Modifying **elements** affects the original. Reassigning the **variable** doesn't.
